 to move.\\n\\n   I went with your explicit instruction (\\\"read operations do not trigger background derivation work\\\") → **reads are uniformly pure** (the same model thread-view's `pull`/`status` already follow). I updated TC-1.5 to the new contract: the first-touch read stays pure (leftover rows untouched, no provider call), and recovery happens the moment the process engages via a non-read path (an explicit drain). It still verifies stranded-work recovery; it no longer claims a *read* drives it.\\n\\n3. **SV-01-003 — process-gate timeout.** The 30 000 ms per-test budget on the CLI parity tests was already in place; `verify-all` runs the inspect process suite in ~2 s with no timeout, and SDK/CLI JSON parity (list ×6 cases, show ×3) deep-equals.\\n\\n**Gates rerun (both green):**\\n- `pnpm verify` (story gate): **28 files / 300 tests** — up from the 290/27 baseline, no regression.\\n- `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1 pnpm verify-all` (epic/process gate): **40 files / 337 tests**, process suite ran.\\n\\n**One thing to flag for your call:** resolving SV-01-001 required editing the Epic-01 TC-1.5 test, because strict read-purity and \\\"a read triggers first-touch catch-up\\\" are mutually exclusive. I asked about this but the prompt was dismissed, so I took your task wording at face value (strictly pure reads). The alternative — **reads pure *except* first-touch recovery** — would leave TC-1.5 untouched but means a read *can* still fire a provider call on a thread's very first open (and would require relaxing the new background read-only test instead). If you'd prefer that direction, say so and I'll switch. I left the pipeline artifacts/impl-log and `red-manifest.json` untouched (the configured gates don't consult the manifest; re-recording is the orchestration tooling's job at acceptance).\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"fc748cdb-c5fb-4e11-a01a-1f0f214d246f\",\"total_cost_usd\":8.991864999999999,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":6781,\"cache_creation_input_tokens